## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a cross-tenant tool governance layer for an LLM-ops gateway with external integrations. It probes policy modeling, isolation, and auditability under realistic latency constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant policy enforcement
- Tool-usage planning and data-scope
- Sandboxed tool execution and per-tenant credentials
- Tamper-evident, signed audit logs
- Latency targets and test plans

## Code Example

```javascript
function evaluatePolicy(prompt, tenantId, toolList) {
  const policy = fetchPolicyForTenant(tenantId);
  // allowlist check
  const allowed = toolList.filter(t => policy.allowedTools.includes(t));
  // data scope check
  const dataOk = policy.dataScope.includes(extractDataScope(prompt));
  // rate limiting
  enforceRateLimit(tenantId, prompt);
  return { allowed, dataOk };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle tool failures or partial failures within a policy?
- How to evolve policies safely across tenants without breaking existing prompts?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to design real-time, scalable guardrails spanning telemetry, model routing, and incident response in multi-tenant LLM gateways, a common production pain point.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming telemetry and per-tenant baselines
- Anomaly scoring combining statistical and rule-based signals
- Remediation policies: throttle, safe-model routing, quarantine
- Auditability and drift handling across tenants
- Data locality and privacy impacts

## Code Example
```javascript
function score(latency, tokens, entropy, baseline) {
  const zLat = (latency - baseline.latency.mean) / baseline.latency.std;
  const zTok = (tokens - baseline.tokens.mean) / baseline.tokens.std;
  const zEnt = (entropy - baseline.entropy.mean) / baseline.entropy.std;
  return Math.max(0, Math.abs(zLat) + Math.abs(zTok) + Math.abs(zEnt));
}
```

